---
export const prerender = false;
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '~/components/Footer.astro';
import PersonalNameText from '~/components/PersonalNameText.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Privacy · Kazon Wilson" description="How this website handles contact messages, audio and software inquiries, resume requests, and information used to keep the site working.">
  <article class="site-shell py-16">
    <div class="max-w-measure">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Updated September 30, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="display mb-6">Privacy</h1>
      <p><PersonalNameText text="This is Kazon Wilson’s website, operated through The Superhuman Group LLC. This notice covers thesuperhuman.us and its audio pages." /></p>
      <h2>Information you send</h2>
      <p>Contact messages, audio inquiries, and resume requests include the details you enter, such as your name, email address, company, and message or project information. These details are used to respond to you, discuss potential work, or review your resume request. Resume delivery requires my approval.</p>
      <h2>Services that process information</h2>
      <p>Cloudflare hosts and protects the site. Its Turnstile service processes browser and network signals to prevent automated form abuse. Resend delivers form notifications and requested emails. Your browser also contacts Google Fonts to load the site’s typefaces.</p>
      <p>Read the providers’ privacy information: <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/policies/privacy/">Cloudflare</a>, <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile</a>, <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, and <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a>.</p>
      <p>For approved audio projects, Stripe processes invoices and payments. The site sends Stripe your name, email address, agreed invoice amount, project reference, project summary and installment description. You enter payment details on Stripe’s hosted invoice page; this website does not collect your card details. The site stores invoice references, payment status and hosted invoice links to manage booking and final delivery. See <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe’s privacy policy</a>.</p>
      <h2>Storage and retention</h2>
      <p>Pending resume requests expire from the website’s request store after seven days and are removed when processed. Temporary IP-based form rate-limit records expire after five minutes. These limits do not delete email copies. Messages and provider logs have separate retention settings; contact me about a specific message or deletion request.</p>
      <h2>Cookies and site operation</h2>
      <p>The site uses hosting and security services to serve pages and investigate failures. The private publishing sign-in uses a short-lived security cookie. The website’s page code does not include advertising trackers. Music pages collect the limited playback information described below.</p>
      <h2>Music and audio projects</h2>
      <p>Music pages record playback starts, progress, 30 seconds of audible listening, completion, and replay. Random identifiers in your browser’s tab storage keep events in order and reduce duplicate counting. Campaign, channel, and creative labels in a link may also be stored so I can compare promotion results. These records describe reported playthroughs, not verified people or audited unique listeners.</p>
      <p>Cloudflare may provide an approximate country, region, and city with a playback request. The site stores that approximation with the event, but does not store the request IP address in the music database. City patterns appear in the private owner view only after at least five qualifying reported sessions share a city. That threshold reduces exposure; it does not prove that five different people listened.</p>
      <p>Individual playback records are reviewed after 90 days. After reviewing a summary, I can remove those records through a manual cleanup. Daily playback totals and privacy-thresholded geography may be retained to understand longer-term interest. Cleanup is not automatic, so individual records may remain longer than 90 days.</p>
      <p>If you request a song or merchandise, the site stores your email, choices, and optional suggestion so I can follow up about that request. This does not create an order or subscribe you to a newsletter. Contact me to withdraw your request or ask for deletion.</p>
      <p>Audio project requests include the file-sharing links and creative notes you provide. Share only files you want me to review. A YouTube lyric video connects to YouTube when you choose to load it; YouTube then handles playback under Google’s privacy policy.</p>
      <p>If an audio project moves into the private studio, the site stores your messages, progress updates, sign-in activity, and private review and final audio. An email code protects access; the site stores a hash of the code and a short-lived session cookie. Final files stop being available one year after publication. After withdrawal or a declined project with no active payment, private studio content becomes eligible for removal after 30 days. Delivered studio content becomes eligible 30 days after its final-file access period and any newer unpublished upload. Old codes and sessions become eligible after 30 days, and non-content security and project audit records become eligible after two years. Cleanup is reviewed and run manually, so records can remain longer than these target periods. Payment and invoice records follow the separate Stripe and owner-request retention process.</p>
      <p>Software project briefs include the project answers you enter, your name, email and optional company, and the timing, budget status and approval details you choose to share. I use them to review your project and reply to you. Please leave out passwords, access details and sensitive personal data. Contact me to withdraw a brief or ask for deletion. After a signed project is completed or access closes, the original request brief and contact details become eligible for reviewed manual cleanup after two years, once private project page content has been removed. Signed agreements and signing evidence follow the separate retention period below.</p>
      <p>If a software project moves to its private project page, the site stores the updates, images and messages shared there, your review decisions, and sign-in activity. An email code protects access, as it does for audio projects. Project page content is kept for the period set out in the signed agreement (one year by default) after the project completes or access closes, then deleted. The page stops being available to you when access closes. Security and project audit records become eligible after two years. Cleanup is reviewed and run manually, so records can remain longer than these target periods.</p>
      <h2>Software agreement signing</h2>
      <p>When website signing is enabled, a one-time email link verifies the offer recipient. Signed agreements, choices, typed names and titles, consent, email verification, IP address and browser information (user agent) are recorded in the signing certificate. Cloudflare stores the private documents and evidence; Resend emails complete PDF attachments to both parties. A short-lived agreement cookie protects review and signing, and a separate archive cookie protects retained downloads.</p>
      <p>Signed agreements, signature records, certificates and attachments included in a signed agreement are retained for 10 years after the agreement ends, longer while an MSA is actively reused or a legal hold applies. Records with no recorded end date remain retained. Uploaded PDF attachments that are not included in an agreement and are no longer referenced by a live offer become eligible for reviewed manual cleanup after 30 days. This archive is separate from project page messages and images, which follow the agreed project retention period. Closing a project page does not remove signed agreements. Agreement access does not restore project access. Contact me to request paper or outside-site signing, or help obtaining a complete copy.</p>
      <h2>Questions or requests</h2>
      <p>To ask about information you have submitted, request access or correction, or request deletion, email <a href="mailto:kazon.wilson@thesuperhuman.us">kazon.wilson@thesuperhuman.us</a>. Include enough context to identify your message without sending sensitive documents.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Footer compact slot="footer" />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.2; margin-block: 2rem .75rem; }
  p { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  a { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
